Thio linkage between CdS quantum dots and UiO-66-type MOFs as an effective transfer bridge of charge carriers boosting visible-light-driven photocatalytic hydrogen production
a State Key Laboratory of Electrical insulation and Power Equipment, Center of Nanomaterials for Renewable Energy, School of Electrical Engineering, Xi'an Jiaotong University, Xi'an 710049, China b Collaborative Innovation Center of Chemistry for Energy Materials, State Key Laboratory of Physical Chemistry of Solid Surfaces, Department of Chemistry, College of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, Xiamen University, Xiamen 361005, PR China
\* Corresponding author E-mail: jianwen.shi@mail.xjtu.edu.cn (J.-W. Shi) wangchengxmu@xmu.edu.cn (C. Wang)
Abstract
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs)/semiconductor hybrids have attracted attention in photocatalysis. Herein, we report a new strategy to use thiol-laced UiO-66 (UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}^{\cdot}$ ) as a porous and functional support for anchoring CdS quantum dots (QDs) (size: $0.5/3~\mathrm{{nm}}$ ). $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ ions are firstly absorbed into the cavities of UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ MOFs via coordinating to the thiol groups in the presence of a base to produce UiO-66-(S-Cd)2, then thiourea is added to form UiO-66-(S-CdS)2 (abbreviated as UiOS-CdS). It is clearly revealed by ultrafast transient absorption spectroscopy that the thio linkage between UiO-66 and CdS acts as an effective transfer bridge of charge carriers, which greatly promotes the interface transfer process of photogenerated electrons and holes, boosting the photocatalytic hydrogen production performance from water splitting. The optimized UiOS-CdS exhibits a photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production rate of $153.2\ \upmu\mathrm{mol\h^{-1}}$ ( $10~\mathrm{mg}$ of catalyst) under visible-light irradiation ( $\lambda>420$ nm) in the absence of nobel metal co-catalyst, corrsponding to an apparent quantum efficiency of $11.9\%$ at $420~\mathrm{nm}$ . This work may provide an effective strategy to construct QDs-linkerMOFs stylephotocatalysts for efficient energy conversion.
Keywords: metal-organic frameworks, thiol-functionalized UiO-66, thio linkage, photocatalysis, hydrogen evolution
## 1. Introduction
Photocatalytic hydrogen evolution from water splitting has received increasing attention as a solution to counter the depletion of fossil fuels and the associated environmental pollution in burning fossil fuels. Efficient and stable photocatalysts are the key towards grand-scale water splitting [1-3]. Until now, a large number of photocatalysts have been developed by scientists, such as metal oxides [4,6], metal sulfides [7-10], $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ [11,12], etc. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), as a class of porous crystalline materials assembled from metal ions/clusters and polyfunctional organic ligands by coordination bonds [13-19], have been widely studied in various application areas, such as gas separation [20,21], heterogeneous catalysis [22-24], chemical sensing [25-27],and nanomedicine [28-30], due to their high specific surface area, tunable pore size, functionalized and designable framework structure. More recently, it has been demonstrated that some MOFs can act as photocatalysts [14, 31-37] and the porous MOF structure facilitates the diffusion of substrates and products in the photocatalytic reactions. The combination of metal centers and organic functionalities allows for fine tuning and rational design of the MOF photocatalysts at molecular level [38]. Moreover, compositing MOFs with other functional materials, such as semiconductors, metal nanoparticles [39-41], enzymes [42, 43], dye molecules [44, 46], organometallic compounds [47, 48], and covalent organic frameworks (COFs) [49, 50], can open up more opportunities for developing new photocatalysts.
MOF is a useful support for semiconductors with several advantages: (i) the MOF nanocavity may constrain the size of semiconductor particles; (ii) the high specific surface area of MOF can lead to abundant catalytic sites; and (iii) the interconnected channels in the host facilitate the transport of photogenerated charge carriers to enhance photocatalytic activity. Several studies on MOF/semiconductor heterostructures have been reported, among which cadmium sulfide (CdS) as one of semiconductors has received special attention due to its lightharvesting ability and size-dependent electronic and optical properties [51-61]. For example, as a special MOF, UiO-66 has been used as substrate to load CdS nanorods for developing new photocatalysts [51]. He et al. reported a CdS-embedded MIL-101 with enhanced visible-light photocatalytic activity [62]. However, the direct deposition of CdS on MOFs could possibly cause the aggregation of CdS nanoparticles, resulting in reduced surface area and the coverage of active sites in MOFs, thus leading to a fast recombination of photogenerated charge carriers [16]. Therefore, carefully designed MOFs/CdS heterostructure with a high conductivity for charge carriers, suppressed recombination, and fast surface reaction kinetics to compete against charge recombination are required to achieve a high photocatalytic efficiency.
It has been confirmed that the functionalization of MOFs with different organic linkers can dramatically affect the properties of the MOFs [63, 64]. Notably, amino-functionalized UiO-66 can promote charge separation and/or extend light absorption of MOFs to the visible region [65-69]. If thiol groups (-SH) are introduced onto the UiO-66 backbone, then metal ions (such as $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ ) are introduced into the MOF cavity via coordination bonds, thus the resultant UiO-66/CdS with thiol groups as linker will be effectively avoided the aggregation of CdS quantum dots, facilitating the enhancement of photocatalytic performance.
In this study, we firstly synthesized UiO-66 MOF with thiol linkers (UiO-66-(SH)2), and then connected $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ ions to the thiol groups in the presence of a base to neutralize generated proton in a post-synthetic modification (UiO-66-(S-Cd)2), followed by adding sulfur source to finally form UiO-66-(S-CdS)2 photocatalyst (abbreviated as UiOS-CdS). The synthesis process of UiOS-CdS is illustrated in Scheme 1. This strategy is different from that of the MOFs/CdS prepared by photo-deposition method, in which the two components lack effective bonding connections. In our work, the strongly coupled UiOS-CdS with thio linkage acting as bridging ligand allowed highly efficient separation and migration of photogenerated charge carriers. As a result, the well-designed catalyst (UiOS-CdS0.7) exhibited a remarkably enhanced photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate of $153.2\ \mathrm{\upmumol\h^{-1}}$ ( $10~\mathrm{mg}$ of catalyst) in the absence of nobel metal co-catalyst, which is much higher than that of a control sample UiO-66-(SH)2/CdS (UiOSH/CdS) without thio linkage under the same conditions $(6.1\upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{h}^{-1}.$ ). More importantly, we well revealed the critical role of the thio linkage in the separation of photogenerated charge carriers by using ultrafast transient absorption spectroscopy. This work provides new insights into designing MOFs/semiconductor composite photocatalysts for application in solar energy conversion.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1 Synthesis of UiO-66-(SH)2
The synthesis of UiO-66-(SH)2 framework was performed in a Schlenk tube ( $25~\mathrm{mL}$ ) with inert gas protection. 96 mg of $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ , $95\mathrm{mg}$ of 2,5-disulfanylterephthalic acid ( $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ -BDC) and $3.4~\mathrm{mL}$ of acetic acid were dissolved in $16~\mathrm{mL}$ of N, N-dimethyl formamide (DMF), then the tube was evacuated and refilled by Ar for 3 times with stirring. The tube was then capped tightly and placed in a pre-heated oil bath at $120~^{\mathrm{oC}}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . After cooling naturally, the obtained sample was centrifuged and washed with anhydrous methanol for several times to remove the residual DMF, then was dried under vacuum at $50^{\mathrm{{o}}}\mathrm{{C}}$ for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ , resulting in UiO-66-(SH)2.
## 2.2 Synthesis of UiOS-CdS nanocomposites
$25\mathrm{\mg}$ of UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ , a certain amount of $\mathrm{Cd}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ , and $15\ \mathrm{\mg}$ of 1,8- Diazabicyclo [5.4.0] undec-7-ene (DBU) (as a base to neutralize proton generated in the reaction between the thiol group and $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ ) were dissolved in $15~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF by sonication for
$20\ \mathrm{min}$ to form a homogeneous solution. Subsequently, the solution was stirred at room temperature for $24\mathrm{h}$ , and then was washed twice with anhydrous methanol. Afterwards, $38~\mathrm{mg}$ of thiourea was added to the solution, which was sonicated for $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ , heated in an oil bath at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and stirred for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The final product was collected by centrifugation, washed with anhydrous methanol, and dryed under vacuum at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $6\textup{h}$ , resulting in UiOS-CdS. For convinence, the as-synthesized UiOS-CdS samples with $100\mathrm{wt\%}$ , $70\mathrm{wt\%}$ and $40\%$ of Cd $(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ were labeled as UiOS-CdS1, UiOS-CdS0.7 and UiOS-CdS0.4, respectively.
## 2.3 Synthesis of UiO-66, UiO/CdS0.7 and pure CdS
For further comparison, we synthesized UiO-66 framework without thiol groups. $125~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ , $5\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF and $1\mathrm{mL}$ of concentrated HCl were added into a Schlenk tube $(25\mathrm{mL})$ ), and were sonicated for 30 minutes until fully dissolved. $123\mathrm{mg}$ of terephthalic acid and $10\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF were then added, and the resultant mixture was sonicated for an additional 30 minutes before being heated in a pre-heated oil bath at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 12 hours. After naturally cooling to room temperature, the resultant suspension was centrifuged and washed twice with anhydrous methanol. The final product was dried under vacuum at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ , resluting in UiO-66. The synthesis of the UiO/CdS0.7 composite was similar to that of UiOS-CdS0.7 composite without thiol groups. The pristine CdS was prepared under the same synthesis conditions as UiOS-CdS without UiO-66-(SH)2 addition.
## 2.4 Synthesis of UiOSH/CdS0.7 and UiOSH-CdS0.7
For comparison, a control sample of UiO-66-(SH)2/CdS0.7 (abbreviated as UiOSH/CdS0.7) was prepared by the same method as UiOS-CdS0.7 in the absence of the DBU agent. Another control sample of UiO-66-(SH) $^{1}2$ -CdS0.7 (abbreviated as UiOSH-CdS0.7) was also prepared by the same method as UiOS-CdS0.7 excepting that $\mathrm{Cd(NO_{3})_{2}\cdot4H_{2}O}$ and thiourea are replaced with already synthesized CdS.
## 2.5. Photocatalytic activity for $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ evolution
The photocatalytic hydrogen evolution test was carried out in a top-irradiation quartz cell connected to a closed glass gas circulation system (CEL-SPH2N, Beijing). $10~\mathrm{\mg}$ of photocatalyst was dispersed in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of aqueous solution with 0.35 M ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{S}}{\cdot}9{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $0.25\mathrm{M}$ ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{3}$ as sacrificial agent (note: without noble metal co-catalyst addition). The light was provided by a 225 W Xenon lamp (CEL-PF300-T8) coupled with a UV cut-off filter ( $420<\lambda$ $<780~\mathrm{nm}$ ). The amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolved under irradiation was determined via a TCD gas chromatograph (GC-9720).
## 3. Results and discussion
The PXRD patterns of pristine CdS, UiO-66-(SH)2 and UiOS-CdS0.7 nanocomposites are shown in Fig. 1a, and the PXRD patterns of UiOS-CdS samples with different mass ratios of CdS, UiO/CdS0.7 and UiOSH/CdS0.7 are displayed in Fig.1b. The PXRD pattern of the UiO$66\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ corresponds well to the simulated one, demonstrating its successful synthesis. The diffraction peaks of the pure CdS are identical to those of the hexagonal CdS (JCPDS No. 41- 1049). All the peak positions of the UiOS-CdS composites are in good agreement with those of the UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ , suggesting that the introduction of CdS has not affect the structure of UiO$66\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ . The intensity of the first peak of UiOS-CdS composites is significantly reduced, which matches with the simulated PXRD pattern of UiOS-CdS (CdS in the MOF cavity). The very broad scattering centered at $26^{\mathrm{o}}$ in the PXRD patterns of UiOS-CdS composites can be attributed to the (002) diffraction of CdS confined in the nanocavities of the UiO-66 MOFs with a diameter below $1\mathrm{nm}$ . In comparison, the UiO-66/CdS0.7 (UiO/CdS0.7, without thio linkage) does not show these changes in PXRD pattern, suggesting that thiol groups are important for CdS quantum dots to be absorbed into the cavity of UiO-66 MOFs. In addition, the UiOSH/CdS0.7 prepared without DBU addition did not show these changes in PXRD pattern, indicating that, as a base to neutralize proton generated in the reaction between the thio-group and $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ , the DBU is critical for the efficient connection of $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ onto the thiol groups, and thus the generation and stabilization of CdS in the cavity of UiO-66 MOFs.
To reveal the presence of -SH groups in the samples, we recorded IR and Raman spectra of UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ , UiOS-CdS0.7, UiOSH/CdS0.7, UiO/CdS0.7, and UiO-66. As shown in Fig. S1 and S2, a new peak at $2560~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ appears in the both IR and Raman spectra of the UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ in comparison with UiO-66, which is consistent with the S-H stretching frequency of thiol groups, suggesting that thiol groups have been successfully introduced in $\mathrm{UiO-}66\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ . However, this peak becomes nearly absent in UiOS-CdS0.7, indicating the cleavage of S-H bonds due to the formation of new S-Cd bonds. In contrast, the peak at $2560~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ remains almost unchanged in the IR and Raman spectra of the UiOSH/CdS0.7, which reveals that no bond is formed between UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ and CdS in this sample due to the absence of DBU [70- 72]. In addition, the elemental composition of the UiOS-CdS0.7 was determined by ICP-MS, which reveals that the ratio of Zr (from UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}\mathrm{,}$ ) to Cd is around 1:0.68.
The morphologies of the UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ , pure CdS, and UiOS-CdS0.7 were investigated by TEM. As shown in Fig. S3a-b, the UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ exhibits polyhedral morphology with particle size about $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . The pristine CdS shows nonuniform nano and even sub-micron sphere shape with serious agglomeration (Fig. S3c-d). As displayed in Fig. 2a and b, a large numbe of CdS quantum dots are uniformly encapsulated within the cavity and/or attached on the surface of UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ without any agglomeration. The lattice fringe of $0.32\ \mathrm{nm}$ corresponding to (101) plane of CdS can be observed in the HRTEM image of UiOS-CdS0.7 (Fig. 2b). The component and structure of UiOS-CdS0.7 were further analyzed by the EDS mapping (Fig. 2c-2h) and size distribution for CdS in UiOS-CdS0.7(Fig. S4), which gave a slightly different distribution of Cd as compared to the other elements, suggesting the presence of larger CdS particles about 3nm on the external surface, and the presence of smaller CdS particles about $1\mathrm{nm}$ in the cavities because of space limitation. We can also observe CdS QDs of similar size on the external surface in the TEM image of UiOSH/CdS0.7 (Fig. S5a-c), but rarely. The CdS particles on the external surface of UiO/CdS with the diameter of approximately $10\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. S5d-f), which are much larger than that of UiOS-CdS0.7, suggesting that the thiol groups in MOFs must provide important binding sites to stabilize and immobilize the CdS QDs with smaller diameter.
To provide further evidence for the occupation of the cavities in UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ by CdS QDs, the $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption-desorption measurement was carried out to compare the pore volume before and after the loading of CdS QDs. As shown in Fig. 3a, the isotherm of UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ belongs to the typical type I according to the IUPAC classification, indicating its typical microporous structure. The bare CdS displays a type IV isotherm with a H3 typical hysteresis loop, suggesting the presence of mesopores [73]. These mesopores may come from the interstitial voids between CdS quantum dots. All UiOS-CdS samples exhibit hybrid type I/IV isotherms with obvious hysteresis between adsorption and desorption branches, revealing the combination of micropores and mesopores [52]. The coexistence of both micropores and mesopores can be confirmed by the corresponding pore size distributions. As shown in Fig. 3b, the micropore size distribution of UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ calculated on the basis of density functional theory (DFT) reveals two major pores at 0.3 and $0.5\ \mathrm{nm}$ . As the loading amount of CdS increases, pore volume gradually decreases, indicating that the cavities of MOFs have been filled. As shown in Table S1, the pristine UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ possesses a large BET specific surface area $(\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{BET}})$ of $521.3\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ . All UiOS-CdS samples exhibit smaller $\mathrm{S_{BET}}$ (from $198.8\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ to 77.7 $\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ than that of the pristine UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ , and the $\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{BET}}$ values of the samples decrease with the increasing CdS content. Similarly, the total pore volumes of UiOS-CdS samples also display a decline with the increasing ratio of CdS to UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ $\mathrm{(from}0.4829\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ of pristine UiO$66\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ to $0.2641\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ of UiOS-CdS0.4). A much less decrease of the surface area or pore volume was observed for the UiOSH/CdS0.7 $(\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{BET}}=447.2~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , $\mathrm{V_{pore}=0.4099~c m^{3}/g)}$ , suggesting that the micropores in the UiOSH/CdS0.7 are not filled with CdS QDs.
The thermal stability of the samples was evaluated by thermogravimetric analysis (TG(A) under $\mathrm{O}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ atmosphere. As displayed in Fig. S6, a weight loss of UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ in the temperature range of $25{\mathrm{-}}120~^{\mathrm{{o}C}}$ can be attributed to the removal of adsorbed water. The framework decomposition begins at around $300^{\mathrm{{o}C}}$ , resulting in thermally stable residues at around $600^{\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{C}$ . When CdS is present, a weight loss between 200 and $300~^{\mathrm{o}}\mathrm{C}$ is observed and can be ascribed to the removal of $S^{2-}$ ions [74]. The residual weights at $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for the UiOSCdS samples match the different loading levels of the CdS [75].
XPS analysis was carried out to investigate the electronic state of sulfur in the obtained UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ and UiOS-CdS0.7. As shown in Fig. 4, the S 2p core-level spectrum of UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ shows two peaks at 163.4 and $164.6~\mathrm{eV}$ , which verifies the presence of the thiol group [70, 76-78]. The UiOS-CdS0.7 shows five characteristic peaks, the energy peaks at 164.6 and $163.5\mathrm{eV}$ match with the $\mathrm{S}_{2\mathrm{p}}$ of the -SH group, and the energy peaks at 162.30 and $161.4\mathrm{eV}$ are consistent with the S 2p of CdS, whereas the third peak at $168.2\mathrm{eV}$ implies that a small amount of sulfur is in an oxidized state [70-72]. Fig. S7 shows the binding energies of Cd $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and Cd $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ at 404.8 and $411.6\mathrm{eV}$ , respectively, which are typical for $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ in CdS [79, 80]. As shown in Table S2, their percentages were close to their dosages.
The optical absorption properties of the samples were investigated by UV-vis absorption spectra (Fig. 5a), and the corresponding band gaps were further determined by fitting the absorption edges using the Tauc/David−Mott model (Fig. S8a) [81]. As shown in Fig. 5a, the pristine UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ shows a strong absorption peak at around $355\mathrm{nm}$ with an absorption edge at $450\ \mathrm{nm}$ induced both by the charge transfer from O to $Z\mathbf{r}$ in the $\mathrm{Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}}$ clusters and the influence of the -SH linker [63]. For CdS, an absorption edge at $550\mathrm{nm}$ can be found. As regard to UiOS-CdS samples, the absorption edges have a large red shift, located between UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ and CdS. The flat band position $(\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{F(B)}}$ values of CdS and UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ estimated via the Mott-Schottky equation at $1/C^{2}=0$ (Fig. S8b-c) are -0.46 V, -0.85 V vs. NHE, respectively, thus corresponding conductor band (C(B) of CdS is approximately $-0.56\mathrm{~V~}$ vs. NHE (Fig. S8b), and the valence band (V(B) position of CdS as determined by $\mathrm{E_{VB}=E_{C B}+E g}$ is 1.87 eV vs. NHE [82-85]. Similarly, the LUMO and HOMO values of UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ are - 0.95 and 1.93 V vs. NHE, respectively (Fig. S8c) [86].
The photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution performances were investigated over these samples in the presence of $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ and ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{3}$ as sacrificial agents without nobel metal co-catalyst under visible-light light irradiation $\lambda{>}420~\mathrm{nm},$ . As shown in Fig. 5b and Fig. S9, the pristine UiO$66\mathrm{-}(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ exhibits a negligible $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate, indicating that UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ can not act as an effective photocatalyst by itself. Notably, upon incorporating CdS into UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ , the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate over UiOS-CdS0.7 is enhanced to $153.2\ \mathrm{\textmumol\h^{-1}}$ (corresponding to 15.32 mmol $\mathrm{h^{-1}~g^{-1}}$ because $10~\mathrm{mg}$ of catalyst is use(d), which is 7.5 times that of the CdS $(20.5~\upmu\mathrm{mol}~\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ . A further increase in the CdS content results in a decline in the photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate, which may be attributed to the block of the channels of the MOF. The remarkable $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate of UiOS-CdS0.7 demonstrates the synergistic effects of UiO-66 and CdS in boosting the photocatalytic performance via the thio linkage. Moreover, the poor photocatalytic activity displayed by the UiOSH-CdS0.7 $(15.3\ \mathrm{\textmumol\h^{-1}}$ ), UiOSH/CdS0.7 $(6.1\ \mathrm{\textmumol\h^{-1}}$ ) and UiO/CdS $(1.5\upmu\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ ) further highlights the critical role of thio linkage between CdS and UiO-66 MOF for efficient photocatalysis. The apparent quantum efficiency (AQE) values over UiOS-CdS0.7 at 420 and $380\mathrm{nm}$ are determined to be $11.9\%$ and $14.8\%$ , respectively (Fig. 5c). We carefully consulted the hydrogen evolution rate over different metal sulfide/MOF-based photocatalysts. reported in literatures (Table S3), and found that the hydrogen evolution rate over UiOS-CdS in this work was far higher than similar binary heterojunction photocatalysts in the absence of cocatalyst. In addition, UiOS-CdS0.7 can be reused for at least four consecutive cycles without significant changes in activity (Fig. 5d). Furthermore, the structural integrity of the UiOSCdS0.7 is still maintained after the reaction, as evidenced by the PXRD patterns (Fig. S10). We further measured the morphology of the UiOS-CdS0.7 after 48 hours photocatalytic reaction. As shown in Fig. S11, the morphology of the UiOS-CdS0.7 remained intact, and no serious agglomeration of CdS QDs occurred, further confirming the stability of the UiOS-CdS0.7.
To gain deeper insight into the mechanism of the enhanced photocatalytic activity, EIS measurement and transient photocurrent response as well as time-resolved fluorescence were conducted to reveal the separation and recombination behaviors of photogenerated electronhole pairs. Fig. 6a shows the EIS results of these samples. The UiOS-CdS0.7 displays the smallest semicircle radius among these samples, indicating it possess the lowest interfacial charge-transfer resistance. Furthermore, the pristine UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ and CdS show negligible photocurrent response owing to the quick recombination of photogenerated charge carriers (Fig. 6b). In contrast, a much higher photocurrent is obtained by the UiOS-CdS0.7. This remarkable improvement may be attributed to an enhanced charge mobility benefiting from the formation of UiO-66- thio linkage-CdS structure. Moreover, the UiOS-CdS0.7 also displays a smaller resistance and higher photocurrent than the UiO/CdS and the UiOSH/CdS0.7, implying the thio linkage is very important for the transfer of photogenerated electrons and holes. The results of the EIS and the photocurrent response confirm that the superior charge transfer and the possible inhibition of recombination in the UiOS-CdS0.7 are mainly responsible for the enhancement of
photocatalytic activity.
Fig. 7a-c show the TA spectra of the CdS, UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ and UiOS-CdS0.7 at different delay times after the excitation at $410\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The CdS QDs exhibit a long-lived ground state bleach (GS(B) signal at around $470\ \mathrm{nm}$ and stimulated emission (SE) signal at over $520~\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 7a). The GSB signal (negative signal) over CdS represents the concentration of holes in its valence band, and the SE signal can represent the concentration of free electrons in the conduction band [87-89]. The TA spectrum of UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ exhibits GSB signal at $506~\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 7b) and an SE signal at around $540\mathrm{nm}$ , which overlaps with a strong and broad (450-480 nm, 506-680 nm) excited state absorption (ES(A) signal. The signals of SE and ESA (positive signal) can represent the concentration of free electrons in HOMO [90]. The SE feature decays within the first 20 ps and a quick decreased ESA in the $506{-}680~\mathrm{nm}$ range. This process may be assigned to obtain a triplet excited state of the $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ -BDC ligand for electron transfer.
The TA spectra of the UiOS-CdS0.7 at early times are similar to that of the CdS (Fig. 7c). That is because the $(-\mathsf{S})_{2}$ -BDC is strongly coupled to the CdS QD via the thio-bridge so that the electronic structure of the $(-\mathsf{S})_{2}$ -BDC merged with that of the CdS QD, and the GSB and SE peaks of the $(\mathrm{HS})_{2}$ -BDC are lost. Given that the relaxation kinetics of the TA signal depends on the probing wavelength, we globally fit a set of kinetic traces wavelength at 470 (GS(B)/650 nm (ES(A). As shown in Fig. 7d, an increase of the signal at $650~\mathrm{nm}$ (ESA of the ligand and SE of the CdS) is also observed within 30 ps, with concomitant positive movement of the signal at $470\ \mathrm{nm}$ (GSB of the CdS Q(D). The continuing decay of the time trace at $470\ \mathrm{nm}$ (GSB of the CdS) can be regarded as the electron injection from the $(-\mathsf{S})_{2}$ -BDC to the CdS [91, 92]. The time trace at $650~\mathrm{nm}$ moves to the negative direction because of the removal of the ligand excited state but the retention of the SE of the CdS QD due to free electrons in the conduction band. These electrons can be finally used for HER reaction. The holes are effectively “consumed” by the sacrificial reagents ( $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ and $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{3},$ ) to slow down the recombination between the electron and the hole, leading to efficient photocatalysis. In addition, the optimal sample decays quickly within 30 ps, revealing a greatly efficient charge transfer rate and further confirms the efficient photocatalytic activity. The oxidization of the thio-sites will not show signal in the monitored spectra range (Fig. S13).
On the basis of the above results, the mechanism for photocatalytic hydrogen production can be proposed. As shown in Scheme 2, under visible-light irradiation, UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ and CdS are excited to generate electron-hole pairs. The photo-generated electrons are transferred from the LUMO of UiO-66-S- to the CB of CdS QDs through the thio linkage, and then reduce $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ to produce $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ . The holes are quickly scavenged by the sacrificial agent $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ and $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{3}\mathrm{.}$ ). Therefore, the improved $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution performance of UiOS-CdS can be ascribed to the following reasons: (i) the thiol groups tethered to the backbone of MOFs offer the binding sites to immobilize CdS within the cavities of the MOFs, and thus prevent the aggregation of CdS quantum dots. (ii) the matchable band alignment of UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ and CdS provides strong driving forces for the migration and separation of photogenerated electrons and holes between them. (iii) the thio linkage between CdS and UiO-66-S- as an effecient bridge greatly facilitates the interfacial transfer processes of charge carriers.
