## 4. Conclusions
In summary, we designed and synthesized a series of thiol-laced UiO-66-type framework linked to metal $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ to form UiOS-CdS composite materials for photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution. The resulting UiOS-CdS0.7 material shows excellent photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution rate of 153.2 $\mathsf{\mu}\mathrm{mol\h^{-1}}$ ( $10~\mathrm{mg}$ of catalyst), and the AQE was as high as $11.9\%$ under the irradiation of 420 nm light. Based on our discussion, the excellent photocatalytic activity is attributed to the rationally designed heterostructure where the well dispersed CdS quantum dots are encapsulated in the UiO-66- $(\mathrm{SH})_{2}$ cavities with thio linkage acting as linkers. This structure not only retains sufficient surficial active sites but also accelerates the migration and separation of charge carriers and the diffusion of reactant and product molecules. More importantly, the thioCd bridge plays a critical role in the charge separation, promoting the photocatalytic $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production performance. This design strategy in this study opens up a new way for the construction of efficient MOFs/semiconductor photocatalysts for efficient $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution.
